  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Brokers of Modernity has seen clear scholarly uptake, with 16 OpenAlex citations and a citation profile that places it in the top 10% of works in its field and year. The book has also been actively used, with 4,248 OAPEN downloads, and its subject matter aligns with Sustainable Development Goal 11 on sustainable cities and communities.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Martin Kohlrausch\u2019s Brokers of Modernity is a scholarly book on modernist architecture in East Central Europe that has already established measurable academic reach. The record shows 16 citations in OpenAlex, and the book\u2019s field-normalized standing places it in the top 10% for its year, indicating that it has been taken up steadily by later research rather than remaining a narrowly read monograph.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape suggests that the book is being drawn into interdisciplinary conversations around architecture, urbanization, cultural heritage, and East-Central European history. Recent citing works include studies in architectural history, urban and cultural analysis, and broader historical scholarship, showing that the book\u2019s framing of modernism is useful beyond a single subfield. This pattern is consistent with the book\u2019s own emphasis on the international circulation of modernist ideas and the relationship between architecture and state-building after 1918.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage signals reinforce that the book has found an audience. OAPEN records 4,248 downloads, which points to sustained reading and consultation, including from open-access distribution channels. The book is also represented in library holdings and subject classifications focused on modern architecture, European architecture, and architecture and society, suggesting that it has been positioned for both research and teaching use.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also places the book within an open-access and funded publication context. It is available openly, and the publication is associated with KU Leuven and the Volkswagen Foundation. While public-discourse event data are not available here, the combination of citations, downloads, and disciplinary alignment indicates that Brokers of Modernity has already moved beyond publication into active scholarly circulation, with its strongest impact visible in academic reuse and ongoing consultation.</p>",
